[tool] Make sure to include fileciteturn2file0 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. # NeuralShell — Futureproof Top-Tier UI System Spec for Gemini ## Purpose This brief defines the next-level UI direction for **NeuralShell**. The objective is **not** to make the app merely prettier. The objective is to make NeuralShell: - futureproof - modular - scalable - premium - keyboard-first - operator-grade - visually disciplined - capable of growing for years without turning into clutter This spec is for a **full UI system pass**, not a surface-only styling pass. --- ## Core Directive Design NeuralShell as a **command environment**, not a collection of dashboard panels. It should feel like: - a premium operator workstation - a secure command surface - a serious local AI environment - a product with hierarchy, state clarity, and disciplined control It must **not** feel like: - a generic Electron admin template - a startup dashboard with AI branding - a browser chatbot with extra tabs - a messy internal tool that accidentally became customer-facing --- ## Primary Goal Create a UI system that can absorb future growth in: - modules - tools - telemetry - runtime states - automation surfaces - profile systems - release/integrity data - workspace complexity - platform support without losing clarity, hierarchy, or product identity. --- ## Product Reality to Preserve NeuralShell already behaves like a multi-surface system. The current architecture includes: - onboarding - main workspace - session management - settings and profiles - runtime and integrity - command palette Do **not** collapse NeuralShell into a simple chat UI. The strength of the product is that it already feels like a system. The redesign should preserve that strength while improving: - hierarchy - scanability - density control - premium feel - consistency - future scalability --- # 1. Non-Negotiable UI Principles ## 1.1 One Shell, Many Surfaces NeuralShell should use one persistent shell architecture with stable navigation and stable mental models. Recommended shell structure: - **Top command/status bar** - **Left navigation rail** - **Center primary workspace** - **Optional right inspector / telemetry rail** - **Transient overlays** for palette, dialogs, guided actions, and drill-down tools The shell should remain stable while content changes. ## 1.2 Command Hierarchy Over Feature Clutter Every screen must make it obvious what matters most. Every surface must clearly separate: - **Primary** = current task / active work - **Secondary** = context + actions - **Tertiary** = metadata + telemetry + utilities If everything looks equally important, the UI fails. ## 1.3 Keyboard-First Is Core Keyboard interaction is not an extra feature. It is part of the product identity. NeuralShell should feel fast for power users through: - command palette - global shortcuts - pane switching - list navigation - action triggering - focus mode - workspace switching - session routing ## 1.4 Local Workstation Identity The UI must reinforce that NeuralShell is: - local-first - operator-controlled - runtime-aware - system-conscious - not just “AI chat” That identity should appear in layout, labels, states, and interaction design. ## 1.5 Dense but Disciplined NeuralShell can be information-rich, but not visually chaotic. Goal: - high information value - low scanning friction - minimal wasted motion - no decorative clutter --- # 2. System-Level Design Requirements ## 2.1 Tokenized Design System Create a real design token system. No ad hoc styling. Define tokens for: - background layers - panel layers - surface elevation - border weights - semantic colors - spacing scale - radius scale - shadows - typography - motion durations - focus styles - icon sizes - input/control sizes ### Minimum token groups #### Color tokens - `bg.canvas` - `bg.shell` - `bg.panel.primary` - `bg.panel.secondary` - `bg.panel.tertiary` - `border.subtle` - `border.default` - `border.strong` - `text.primary` - `text.secondary` - `text.tertiary` - `accent.primary` - `accent.secondary` - `state.success` - `state.warning` - `state.error` - `state.info` #### Spacing tokens - `space.2` - `space.4` - `space.8` - `space.12` - `space.16` - `space.20` - `space.24` - `space.32` - `space.40` #### Radius tokens - `radius.sm` - `radius.md` - `radius.lg` - `radius.xl` #### Typography tokens - display - h1 - h2 - h3 - label - body - body-sm - mono-sm - mono-xs ## 2.2 Semantic State System NeuralShell needs a formal visual state language. Required shared states: - idle - active - selected - focused - processing - success - warning - degraded - disconnected - recovered - locked - verified - destructive Each state must have: - color behavior - border behavior - icon behavior - text treatment - optional motion behavior Do not invent one-off state styles per screen. ## 2.3 Component Contract System All modules should obey a common contract. Each major component should define: - title - purpose - primary action - secondary actions - current state - empty state - loading state - error state - compact mode - expanded mode This is essential for scale. --- # 3. Visual Direction ## 3.1 Overall Tone Target tone: - premium - tactical - secure - clean - elite - restrained - system-grade This should feel closer to: - a premium developer tool - a command center - a secure workstation - a modern desktop product It should not feel playful, bubbly, or consumer-chat styled. ## 3.2 Color Direction Preserve the strong NeuralShell DNA: - deep navy / dark graphite foundation - controlled gold or warm signal accents - cold electric-blue framing where useful - semantic states with strict discipline Use accent color sparingly. Accent should signal importance, not decorate. ## 3.3 Border and Panel Language Use panel hierarchy intentionally. Three panel classes: - **Level 1**: active task surface - **Level 2**: supporting tools/actions - **Level 3**: telemetry/meta/utility Differentiate them by: - opacity - border intensity - elevation - background weight - header emphasis Do not let every box have the same visual authority. ## 3.4 Typography Typography should communicate seriousness. Requirements: - tight but readable display type - concise headers - restrained body text - strong monospace usage for runtime/status/system labels - fewer long explanatory paragraphs in the UI itself The interface should feel like an operator reads it in scans, not essays. --- # 4. Global Layout Architecture ## 4.1 Persistent Shell Layout Recommended layout: ### Top Bar Contains: - current model / bridge - current workspace / session - runtime status summary - global actions - command palette trigger ### Left Rail Contains: - core surface navigation - workspace navigation - session access - tools/modules - optional pinned destinations ### Center Workspace Primary work area. This should always be the dominant visual zone. ### Right Inspector Rail Optional. Use for: - telemetry - details - contextual info - inspector panels - runtime state - selected-item metadata ### Overlay Layer Use for: - command palette - onboarding overlays - modal confirmations - quick actions - focus switching - transient workflows ## 4.2 Responsive Behavior Must scale across: - smaller laptop screens - larger monitors - ultrawides - future cross-platform variants Rules: - critical actions must remain accessible - layout should collapse predictably - side rails may become drawers - command palette should remain globally available - no layout should depend on one exact screen width --- # 5. Screen-by-Screen Gold Pass ## 5.1 Onboarding ### Current problem Onboarding risks feeling like setup UI rather than system entry. ### Goal It should feel like entering a serious workstation. ### Requirements - strong first-run framing - clear recommended path - clear advanced path - premium spacing - reduced instructional clutter - confident typography - visible shell behind the onboarding layer - progress model that feels intentional, not tutorial-ish ### Structure - Step header - Current state summary - Recommended action card - Advanced setup tray - Safety/verification messaging - Continue CTA - Skip/return logic only where safe ### UX objective New users should feel: “I am initializing a system,” not “I am filling out app settings.” --- ## 5.2 Main Workspace ### Current problem Too many areas can compete at once. ### Goal Main workspace becomes the undeniable center of gravity. ### Requirements - one dominant primary work zone - reduced top-band noise - clear active task/thread emphasis - cleaner separation of primary vs supporting modules - stronger action hierarchy - fewer equally weighted cards - better use of whitespace and internal rhythm ### Recommended structure - Compact workspace header - Primary active surface - Secondary task/action lane - Optional utility strip - Context-aware right-side inspector ### Rules - the eye should know where to land in under 2 seconds - no more than one dominant primary CTA cluster - supporting actions should not compete with the active work --- ## 5.3 Session Management ### Current problem Could feel like storage/list management instead of continuity control. ### Goal Make sessions feel like operational continuity. ### Requirements - emphasize resume / restore / compare / pin / archive - show state clearly - support dense browsing - support detail drill-down - enable sorting/filtering without clutter - clear semantic distinction between active, archived, restored, and degraded states ### UX objective Users should feel: “My work persists and is governable,” not “I am browsing files.” --- ## 5.4 Settings and Profiles ### Current problem Settings can become a dumping ground. ### Goal Make settings feel like system control, not preference soup. ### Recommended split - frequent controls - runtime/bridge controls - profile controls - workspace controls - advanced/system controls ### Requirements - progressive disclosure - safe defaults clearly indicated - advanced sections separated - profile switching fast and clean - stronger difference between “daily settings” and “dangerous settings” ### UX objective Users should feel: “I can control the system,” not “I am lost in app preferences.” --- ## 5.5 Runtime and Integrity ### Current problem Important differentiator, but can easily become visually dense. ### Goal This becomes a premium runtime cockpit. ### Structure - top summary band - state cards - telemetry section - integrity / verification section - incidents / warnings section - logs / details section ### Requirements - instantly scannable current status - pass/degraded/warning/verified states obvious - incidents prioritized by severity - no debug sludge aesthetics - logs readable and collapsible - summary first, depth second ### UX objective Users should feel: “I can trust and inspect the system,” not “I’m staring at raw diagnostics.” --- ## 5.6 Command Palette ### Current problem Strong feature, but should become signature UX. ### Goal This becomes one of NeuralShell’s flagship interaction layers. ### Requirements - fast fuzzy search - grouped commands - module-aware commands - recent commands - suggested commands - scoped prefixes - premium keyboard guidance - elegant empty states - richer command metadata without clutter ### Interaction expectations - keyboard-first navigation - near-zero lag feel - obvious confirm/cancel behavior - visible scope - consistent action previews ### UX objective Users should feel: “I can drive the whole system from here.” --- # 6. Interaction Standards ## 6.1 Motion Use restrained motion for: - state change - surface entry - modal/palette open-close - confirm/restore actions - warning feedback - focus transitions Avoid decorative motion. Motion should communicate: - hierarchy - continuity - confidence ## 6.2 Focus States Focus must be visible and premium. Requirements: - strong keyboard focus ring - never invisible - consistent across buttons, lists, inputs, palette results, panels ## 6.3 Dense Mode + Focus Mode Support at least two interface modes: ### Dense Mode - information rich - faster scanning - lower padding - more visible system detail ### Focus Mode - reduced distractions - centered task surface - hidden tertiary UI - optimized for deep work This is a major futureproofing feature. --- # 7. Accessibility Requirements Top tier means accessible. Required: - full keyboard navigation - high contrast safety - scalable text - reduced motion support - clear semantic states - accessible forms - clear focus indicators - responsive hit targets where needed Accessibility is not optional polish. It is part of elite quality. --- # 8. Futureproofing Requirements ## 8.1 Design for Module Growth New modules must fit existing shell logic and component contracts. No one-off layouts unless absolutely necessary. ## 8.2 Design for Platform Expansion Even if Windows is primary now, the UI should anticipate: - Mac layout expectations - Linux usage patterns - differing titlebar/window assumptions - varied font rendering - different screen densities ## 8.3 Design for Automation Surfaces Future tools may add: - autonomous actions - agents - more telemetry - more command routing - more diagnostics - more system states The design system must handle added complexity without collapsing into noise. ## 8.4 Design for Long-Term Brand Consistency The UI should be able to produce: - app UI - website/storefront extensions - docs visuals - screenshots - release imagery from the same visual language. --- # 9. Deliverables Gemini Should Produce Gemini should generate a **full Futureproof UI System Spec** with the following: ## A. Design principles - concise system doctrine - top-tier quality bar - what NeuralShell should feel like - what it must avoid ## B. Design token system - color tokens - spacing tokens - radius tokens - typography tokens - shadow tokens - motion tokens ## C. Component system - buttons - cards - status chips - panel headers - forms - tabs - command palette rows - nav rail items - session rows - inspector blocks - telemetry cards ## D. Shell layout spec - desktop shell - compact shell - dense mode - focus mode - responsive behavior ## E. Screen-by-screen redesign specs - onboarding - main workspace - session management - settings and profiles - runtime and integrity - command palette ## F. State behavior matrix Map common product states to: - color - border - icon - motion - priority ## G. Interaction spec - shortcuts - palette interactions - focus behavior - transitions - hover strategy - selection behavior ## H. Accessibility checklist - keyboard - contrast - reduced motion - readable hierarchy - target sizes ## I. Futureproofing checklist - module growth support - cross-platform support - telemetry growth support - automation support - visual consistency rules --- # 10. Output Style for Gemini Gemini should produce: - structured markdown - no fluff - no vague design clichés - no generic “clean modern UI” filler - concrete rules - concrete hierarchy decisions - concrete component behavior - concrete layout standards Tone: - elite - precise - system-level - product-grade - future-aware --- # 11. Final Directive Do not redesign NeuralShell into a prettier chatbot. Redesign it into a **futureproof operator workstation** with: - stronger hierarchy - sharper scanability - stable shell architecture - scalable module contracts - keyboard-first prestige - premium runtime surfaces - disciplined visual identity The result should feel like a product that can scale for years without losing itself.
